WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.350 --> 00:00:03.520
Good afternoon.

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:10.590
Welcome to the very short NCBI minute on alerts
for new data.

00:00:10.590 --> 00:00:17.360
As per usual, this recording as well as the
slides will be available both in the YouTube

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:21.430
channel, the webinars playlist and on the
FTP site.

00:00:21.430 --> 00:00:23.410
You can find the links here.

00:00:23.410 --> 00:00:27.930
I will give you a minute to copy the links
down.

00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:29.190
(https://go.usa.gov/xXqGG)

00:00:29.190 --> 00:00:34.390
So over the next few minutes we will talk
about setting up alerts for new data using

00:00:34.390 --> 00:00:35.390
My NCBI.

00:00:35.390 --> 00:00:42.769
I am Ben Busby, the bioinformatics training
lead here at NCBI, and I have with me Peter

00:00:42.769 --> 00:00:48.359
Cooper who is the head of the NCBI education
group.

00:00:48.359 --> 00:00:52.379
Many of you most likely use NCBI literature
resources.

00:00:52.379 --> 00:01:00.109
Many folks are very used to using My NCBI
to do regular literature searches.

00:01:00.109 --> 00:01:07.830
However many of our users do not know that
you can also save searches for data.

00:01:07.830 --> 00:01:11.090
For example, for new assemblies or annotations.

00:01:11.090 --> 00:01:14.579
For new Geo data sets or new SRA data sets.

00:01:14.579 --> 00:01:21.930
I will walk you through how to do that over
the next few minutes.

00:01:21.930 --> 00:01:30.350
The majority of you probably know that MyNCBI
is simply a way to have an account at NCBI.

00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:34.479
It's trivial to sign up for a MyNCBI account.

00:01:34.479 --> 00:01:42.320
In fact, when I was preparing this webinar
I signed up for a MyNCBI account.

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:47.509
Simply so I could have a fairly clean demonstration.

00:01:47.509 --> 00:01:51.189
It's reasonably an easy thing to do.

00:01:51.189 --> 00:01:54.270
There are many folks out there with MyNCBI
accounts.

00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:59.439
I encourage you to get one.

00:01:59.439 --> 00:02:06.869
Creating alerts from any search string at
NCBI is simple.

00:02:06.869 --> 00:02:16.700
Really all one needs to do is click the create
alert button below the search they are already

00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:17.700
doing.

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:20.470
Say I was searching Geo for Alzheimer's and
human.

00:02:20.470 --> 00:02:25.530
You may only have one data series as shown
here.

00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:28.850
I can click the create alert button.

00:02:28.850 --> 00:02:31.910
The alert dialog box would come on.

00:02:31.910 --> 00:02:36.640
I can modify my search term and I can name
my saved search.

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:41.190
I typically use the defaults but if you do
a lot of these I could see that you want to

00:02:41.190 --> 00:02:44.040
name them in a more systematic way.

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:46.880
You can either keep the filters or not.

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:53.930
There are a bunch of ways you can get email
updates.

00:02:53.930 --> 00:02:58.930
This is really quite simple to use and a lot
of people have told us they would like to

00:02:58.930 --> 00:03:05.010
see this service which in fact exists.

00:03:05.010 --> 00:03:10.380
As I mentioned earlier, you can also get alerts
for bacterial annotation and assemblies.

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:15.430
This is useful in my opinion, in the microbial
realm.

00:03:15.430 --> 00:03:23.150
For example, if I want to see new releases
of Salmonella, either assemblies or annotations,

00:03:23.150 --> 00:03:27.670
I can do this search in the NCBI Assembly
database.

00:03:27.670 --> 00:03:37.080
I believe, the NCBI Assembly database is a
very clean and useful resource for finding

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:40.910
genome assemblies.

00:03:40.910 --> 00:03:46.760
Also if you were to do SRA searches, for example
for meta-genomes, you could do that.

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:50.090
The search I did here was for digestive decontamination.

00:03:50.090 --> 00:03:57.970
It's a fascinating thing that is sometimes
done to folks, patients in intensive care

00:03:57.970 --> 00:03:59.280
units.

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:03.140
Here you can see a time course of such experiments.

00:04:03.140 --> 00:04:08.800
Interestingly enough, you can blast into this
time course to look into different bacterial

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:16.590
gene loci that return to the gut after this
massive course of antibiotics.

00:04:16.590 --> 00:04:23.230
I can save this search simply by clicking
on the create alert icon.

00:04:23.230 --> 00:04:30.100
Another nice thing that MyNCBI will do for
you that many folks don't know about, is it

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:32.460
will save custom tracks.

00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:39.840
Both track configurations that you can set
up in the NCBI genome data viewer by clicking

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:45.130
on the Tracks button, but also data that you
upload.

00:04:45.130 --> 00:04:48.870
Here I uploaded a file called test.bed.

00:04:48.870 --> 00:04:55.280
And MyNCBI will save that.

00:04:55.280 --> 00:04:59.240
As with all of our resources, we have documentation.

00:04:59.240 --> 00:05:03.260
You can get those from the NCBI Learn page.

00:05:03.260 --> 00:05:10.460
If you Google for NCBI webinars, it is an
easy way to find the webinars page.

00:05:10.460 --> 00:05:19.070
My favorite way to get to this documentation
page is to go to NCBI/learn and you will be

00:05:19.070 --> 00:05:21.090
able to go to the documentation.

00:05:21.090 --> 00:05:26.460
Once again, an updated version of these slides
will appear on the FTP site in approximately

00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:28.070
one hour from now.

00:05:28.070 --> 00:05:32.850
Some of these links will be more simple.

00:05:32.850 --> 00:05:37.470
Another thing I do in my position at NCBI
is I run Hackathons.

00:05:37.470 --> 00:05:43.970
We have a bunch of them coming up and if you
are pretty familiar with Linux and can do

00:05:43.970 --> 00:05:48.780
some basic scripting, if you are interested
in bioinformatics or medical informatics,

00:05:48.780 --> 00:05:51.660
you should consider coming to one.

00:05:51.660 --> 00:05:54.190
These are the Hackathons that are coming up.

00:05:54.190 --> 00:06:01.530
Some of them look very interesting and there
are some shaping up for spring of 2018.

00:06:01.530 --> 00:06:06.360
In my group I also have intensive internships
for grad students, postdocs and clinicians.

00:06:06.360 --> 00:06:11.970
You can come here to work for 4 to 6 weeks
on either your project or ours, in a collaborative

00:06:11.970 --> 00:06:17.500
environment and leverage NCBI resources.

00:06:17.500 --> 00:06:21.970
We ask that you document the stumbling blocks
that you run in to at NCBI.

00:06:21.970 --> 00:06:28.520
If that is something you're interested, in
please email bioinformatics-training@ncbi.nlm.nih.gov.

00:06:28.520 --> 00:06:35.260
With that, what I want to do is take some
questions, any questions you may have.

00:06:35.260 --> 00:06:39.450
If there are questions that I do not know
the answer to, we will either answer them

00:06:39.450 --> 00:06:44.600
on the FTP site or Peter Cooper can answer
them in person.

00:06:44.600 --> 00:06:49.160
I see no questions in the question pod.

00:06:49.160 --> 00:06:55.010
It's obviously a pretty simple and straightforward
NCBI minute.

00:06:55.010 --> 00:06:59.760
If there are any questions I will give you
the next minute or so to type them in.

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:02.420
If not, I hope you all have a good day.

00:07:02.420 --> 00:07:04.980
A fantastic rest of the week.

00:07:04.980 --> 00:07:06.710
Thank you for coming to this NCBI webinar.

00:07:06.710 --> 00:07:13.260
If you have feedback about the webinar, you
would like to see more, or would like us to

00:07:13.260 --> 00:07:20.180
dwell longer on a specific thing, please feel
free to email webinars@ncbi.nlm.nih.gov or

00:07:20.180 --> 00:07:23.620
put those comments right here in the questions
pod.

00:07:23.620 --> 00:07:24.620
Fantastic.

00:07:24.620 --> 00:07:26.710
I don't see any questions.

00:07:26.710 --> 00:07:28.570
Thank you to those who said thank you.

00:07:28.570 --> 00:07:32.670
Like I said, I hope everyone has a fantastic
week.

00:07:32.670 --> 00:07:35.510
Good luck with your research or whatever else
it is you do.

